// A Bow-Shot Between Them (The Odyssey, Homer; member 5 of 6 of a made series flown from the bow of one dark ship).
//
// A PLACE THAT STANDS. One line of 400 m (the metres 20.2 to 420.3), flown in 70 s at THREE TYPED SPEEDS (PACE_IN, PACE_CROSS, PACE_SLOW; the seconds are what the length and the speeds make them):
// 6.0 m/s over the way in, to the shoal's last ledge abeam; 11.5 m/s over THE OPEN CROSSING (eased up to where the great fallen block is abeam, held to where the low rock's entry end is abeam, eased
// down by where the throat bears 85 degrees: 13 s from the shoal's last ledge to the throat abeam); 4.5 m/s from there on, past the pool and through the gate. The lens rides 3.6 m over the sea and dips
// with it, through a strait in late gold light, THE SKY AWAY FROM THE SUN A DEEP EVENING BLUE with small heaped clouds in it, their heads toward the sun rose: THE HOUR'S ONE SUN STANDS LOW AND IS SEEN
// (13 degrees high, 42 degrees to port of the long straight's bow; the part's SUN), its path of light on the sea, a lit and a shaded side on every rock. THE WAY IN COMES DOWN THE ONE LANE OF LIGHT
// THAT THE SUN THROWS THROUGH THE MOUNTAIN (found by plan from the sun's bearing, typed in LINE and said here): the sun stands beyond the mountain on the way in, and between the great body G1 and the
// body R1 a gully lets it through to the sea in a lane 6 to 8 m broad that narrows and shuts 44 m down the line; the line's first straight is laid in that lane (from the east-north-east, heading 40
// degrees to port of the long straight, the metres 20.2 to 128.2; it begins 220 m from G1's foot centre), so that from her first metre THE MOUNTAIN STANDS WHOLE AND BACKLIT AHEAD with the sun's blaze
// in the gully, its rays through the vapour, the cloud's linings alight and its long shadow on the sea to either hand of her. She then passes between the ledges of the shoal, bends once to starboard
// (85 m radius, the metres 128.2 to 187.5) onto her long straight on the mountain's side of the strait (x = -10, the metres 187.5 to 292), then bends TO PORT BY THE SUN'S OWN BEARING (42.0 degrees,
// 85 m radius, the metres 292 to 354.3) past the low rock's tree end (its horn's foot 31.6 m to starboard at the metre 310) onto a last straight of 66.0 m LAID AT THE SUN: the sun stands over her bow
// and over the way out, its path of light runs up the straight to the way out's foot, between the standing slabs of a gate. THE LAST STRAIGHT'S LENGTH IS BY PLACE: the way out stands in the gate's
// last opening, midway between the last slab she passes and the nearer of the pair that stands beyond it (GATE_LAST, GATE_BEYOND; see LINE).
// On the left hand ONE MOUNTAIN OF DARK BEDDED STONE: one great body (G1) and six lesser ones joined to it, each an irregular prism of leaning faces broken at knees and shelves, the beds dipping through
// all of them; no body ends in a point and no top is level; the great slab shows its beds and a talus at its foot; low fallen blocks at every foot; in the gully's back wall an open forked cleft, nothing
// in it. IT GOES UP INTO A BANK OF CLOUD about its peak: soft puffs (cards turned to the lens as they are drawn; where each is, is the plan's and the clock's), each a heap of round heads with a lit and
// a shaded side, creased where they meet, its foot one ragged ceiling with scallops and thin hanging shreds, its heads' edges alight where the sun stands beyond; the bank's foot comes down over every top
// (66 to 71 m over the sea), vapour lies down the gullies between the bodies, and the bodies about the peak turn slowly on the world clock. THE BANK IS PART OF THE SKY: about the dark heart over the
// summit a broad pale SHELF of soft bodies in three rings (to 250 m from the peak's axis, 90 to 140 m up, thinner and flatter outward) wraps before and behind the summit in the sky's own cloud
// colours and thins into the sky's own bars; the heart alone is dark, and no puff stands alone as a round dark ball.
// Beside the way in THE SHOAL (the part's FANG_AT, SHOAL_DIP, SHOAL_RAMPS): she passes BETWEEN its ledges. To starboard FIVE WET RAMPS (3 to 4.6 m high, 6 to 9 m broad): each a ledge whose top is ONE
// BEDDING PLANE rising with the beds' one dip to a broken scarp at its high end, long along the dip, so that its wet top catches the low sky and the sun's own colour (a sheen by the eye's angle to
// the stone, on the shoal's stone only), with torn white water round its foot in lace that rides with the swell, and five gulls over three of them (SHOAL_GULLS; their rounds 3 m and more over the
// stone and 10 m and more from the lens). To port three low humps awash (2.2 to 2.4 m) and, toward the mountain, the spur's ledges (two of them 5 m high; all are humps of the one law, none a stepped
// stack). The nearest stone is 12.7 m from the lens; the least air to her whole breadth is 7.8 m. THE SHOAL'S STONE IS THE MOUNTAIN'S BEDDED STONE (the part's stone shader, uLift): each flag its own
// tone, its parting darker with a step of the face's slope at it, breaks across the flags, a wet dark foot; its shade a cool grey-blue no more saturated than the mountain's shade; its wet top gives
// back a quarter of a greyed sky and the sun's glow.
// On the right, a bowshot off, A LOW BRIGHT ROCK in unlike stretches (a fluted face, a slumped green slope, a ledged slope, a cut bay, a broken west end; weed in tufts at its foot, broken foam on
// its waterline). ITS RIDGE ENDS TOWARD THE LINE AS A BLUFF (the part's HORN: the rock's own cliff carried round its end: great beds stepping back, thinner beds between them, flutes, ledges that die
// out, three leaning clefts, a broken brink, surf and a wet band at its foot), and on a shelf of the bluff's north-west shoulder, 11 m over the sea, stands ONE GREAT FIG in full leaf: A TREE: a thick
// pale bole leaning out toward the line, five great boughs forking, a crown of unlike leaf masses with gaps of sky between them (its furthest leaf 8.5 m out over the water), seven great roots going
// out from its foot over the shelf, over the brink and down the face (pale bark, lit as the stone is). Under the low rock THE POOL: a standing funnel in the sea, 96 m in radius, teal, then blue, then
// blue-black down an uneven throat to a floor of dark ground; a standing lip on the entering side with white water over it, torn tongues of foam drawn inward. THE POOL IS ONE THING AT EVERY
// DISTANCE: nothing of it reads how far off the eye is; its finest white water (the lip's strands, the tongues, the wall's threads) goes to its own mean where a pixel's footprint on the water is
// broader than it (fineness alone, by the water's own place). ITS WHITE WATER LIES ON THE WATER: torn foam on the lip's standing crest (clots, then strands going in and round with the turn), the
// tongues down the wall, broken water at the low rock's foot; in the air only thrown drops and a low mist, nothing higher than 2 m (no upright card of spray, no gout, no curtain of drops: see THE
// SPRAY in build). THE LOW ROCK'S FOOT along the pool is broken (weed in tufts, a wet gradient, foam in stretches): no dark band. On the world clock (one round in 26 s) the throat is drawn down and
// wells up again. The line passes 52 m from its centre. SEVEN PIECES OF DRIFT TIMBER ride the pool's arms round and go under: PLAIN BOARDS, seven pieces of broken planking (4.4 to 9.0 m long, 1.1 to
// 2.0 m broad, none longer than 4.6 times its breadth), lying flat and low on the water they ride, each on a closed round of the clock, under water at both ends of it. NOTHING ELSE IS ON THE WATER:
// no wreck, no mast, no spar; the open crossing is open water.
// Beyond the low rock THE GATE: THIRTEEN STANDING SLABS of its stone (the part's TAIL, laid from metres along and aside of the last straight). FOUR ARE PASSED: two to starboard as the look comes
// forward from the fig (9.5 and 12 m high, 28 and 42 m off), one to port (12.5 m high, 21 m off) and THE TALLEST OF THE FOUR to starboard (21 m high, 24 m off, abeam at the metre 382), up whose lit
// rim the look goes once. NINE STAND BEYOND THE WAY OUT to either hand of it and are never passed (14 to 30 m high; the nearest two 34 m to either hand of the line and 32 and 34 m beyond the way out,
// the others behind and outside them, to 170 m aside and 184 m beyond): THE GATE GROWS TALLER AND FULLER AS THE WAY NEARS. Each is one standing body by a law of its own round its own leaning axis (the
// part's mkSlab: bedded, fluted, cleft, going in as it goes up, its top broken in unequal levels with one sloping shoulder, no thin upright on any), unlike in height, breadth, lean and turn, RISING
// OUT OF THE SEA on an apron of broken stone, with a wet band, surf in broken stretches and a broken double at its foot, under the gate's own light (the low sun on the faces turned to it, the sky's
// warm and cool light in their shade, a lit edge against the sun, the air's haze with distance: the stone's shader, gate); gulls of a gull's own size wheel round the tallest's rim and round and over
// the others. (The eighth row of the part's TAIL, a small rock before the gate, is out of the world by the owner's bound: STONE_TAIL7_OUT.)
// Nothing is in the cleft. Nobody is shown. Nothing knows the ship passes.
//
// THE VEHICLE: the bow of the series' ship (src/parts/bronze-walled-island.js, buildShip) is THE DECLARED VEHICLE of this world. It is written once, in the head member's part file, and only dressed
// here: her sail set, her oars working, her sway on the world clock. update() gives her the lens's place and the route's heading (eased by place about the two ends of the line's bend to STARBOARD, and there only: see THE HEADING HANDED TO HER below and shipHeading in build), the camera for her own one law (whole or not at all: where the gaze
// turns aside or lifts she is lowered under the look; she comes back as a prow lying across under the look ahead, never as a single tip), THE PACE AT HER OWN METRE for her bow's foam (PACE.speedAt at the
// line's metre nearest the lens: how strong the foam is by that speed is her own law) and ctx.through (true while this world is the next one, seen through a ring: she is not drawn while it is true).
// HER LIGHT IS THE WORLD'S (SHIP_LIGHT in build): the low sun and the
// sky's paled light at 2.6 each, so that her deck is warm planked wood and her rail and stem cool against the sun. Her sway is eased BY PLACE: from nought over the first 40 m, to nought over the last
// 40 m, fullest in the narrows; and her sun is put out BY HER PLACE where she lies in the high rock's shadow (the sea's own shadow law, read at the lens). update() reads nothing else of the visitor
// (the lens's place; the share of the route flown, for the heading; the camera, for her one law), and nothing of the place reads the visitor at all.
// THE HEADING HANDED TO HER, in the open. The line has TWO bends: one to starboard (M.bend to M.onto, the metres 128.2 to 187.5) and one to port (LINE.out to M.straight, 292 to 354.3). The heading is
// eased about the STARBOARD bend's two ends only: within HEAD_REACH (17.92 m) of either end she is handed the mean of the route's heading over HEAD_SPAN metres, that is from the metre 110.24 to 146.08
// and from 169.58 to 205.42 (0.2251 to 0.3147 and 0.3734 to 0.4630 of the line); everywhere else the route's bare heading, as in live. THE FIRST REACH BEGINS 17.9 m BEFORE THE BEND and before 0.27 of
// the line: over that first part (110.24 to the bend at 128.16) the look is aside on the shoal, she is wholly lowered under it by her own law and nothing of her is drawn; as flown she begins to come
// up at about the metre 139 to 141 and stands whole from about 141 to 144 (the journey's camera and the steady one).
// THE PORT BEND'S TWO ENDS (292 and 354.3) ARE HANDED THE ROUTE'S BARE HEADING, AS IN LIVE. At 292 she is wholly lowered under the look up at the fig. At 354.3 the look's law has left the bow for the
// gate's one side look (32 degrees aside and 8 up there), so the bend ends as she goes down by her own law: as flown she stands whole to 2.6 m before it (the journey's camera) or 0.6 m before it (the
// steady one) and is wholly lowered by 4 m after it. That end falls where her bow is leaving the picture, not under a look held on it; a visitor may still see the last of her there. It is left as
// live has it in this round and stands on the polish list.
// HER WAKE AND FOAM AT THE STARBOARD BEND'S TWO ENDS: she has no wake as a drawn thing, and her foam's mesh is not drawn (the ship's part: not until its colour is a true colour); so nothing parts from her hull
// or trails at an angle there. Where these comments speak of her bow's foam or white water (the lifts of the two turns, the pace handed for it), they speak of that mesh as it lies about the lens and
// as the looks were typed against it, not of anything now drawn.
//
// WHAT IS DRIVEN BY WHAT.
//   By the metres flown: THE GAZE, one smooth law of yaw and pitch; every knot is the line's metre at a NAMED BUILT PLACE (abeam, or bearing a named angle), computed in build (see M, KY, KP, KY2, bowPitch, gazeAt
//     and THE LOOK'S WHOLE TABLE there): THE MOUNTAIN WHOLE AND BACKLIT over her prow; THE SHOAL NEAR, down and aside onto the ledges she passes between; ROUND ONTO THE BOW, held dead ahead over her prow
//     from the shoal's last ledge to the funnel's rim while the line's bend to starboard swings her onto the crossing; then ACROSS THE STRAIT FROM THE SHADOW, by where the low rock's entry end is abeam, to the lit low
//     rock, the fig and the pool's first sight; onto the throat itself, the pool straight before the look as she comes abeam; up at the fig on its brink; then forward in one even
//     turn onto the sun as the gate opens ahead; ONE SIDE LOOK up the tallest passed slab's lit rim as it comes abeam; and back to the bow, the sun over the way out. By the metres flown too: THE PACE (three typed
//     speeds eased between named built places: makePace). NOTHING ELSE OF THE PLACE READS THE METRES (the ship, the vehicle, takes the route's heading there, meaned over HEAD_SPAN metres about the two ends of the bend to starboard, and the pace at her own metre for her foam: see THE VEHICLE). LOW BROKEN
//     WATER STANDS AT THE WAY OUT'S FOOT ALWAYS, a standing thing of the place: there at every metre of the line and every second, from anywhere (its cards on rounds of the world clock); NO LIGHT OF THE OPENING LIES IN THE WATER. The ring itself is the engine's:
//     its late drawing (portal.drawWithin) begins at a named built place of the line (M.shoulder: see THE WAY OUT below), by the route's distance, never by the lens's own distance to anything and
//     never by what is in the picture.
//   By the world clock plus a phase the seed deals, FOUR THINGS ONLY: the pool's round and all that follows the round's own age (the funnel drawn down and thrown up, its white water, the boil's low
//     mist), the drift timber (where on its round the first piece is, and which arm it rides), the birds (each one's place on its round and in its beat) and the ship's sway.
//   By the bare world clock with typed phases, THE SAME ON EVERY SEED: the swell's trains, the surf and the wash at the rocks' feet, the spray's cards (each on a round of its own, its share of it typed by
//     its own number) and the cloud's slow turn and its drifting outline.
//     Each is right at any second from any place of the line; nothing is played once.
//   By place (standing): the light (one fixed hour; the sun's visibility baked on every skin; the mountain's long shadow on the sea by the law of its faces), the wetness of the stone, the layout.
//   By the seed (once), each in a stream of its own: the round's phase and small things only (the low rock's relief and sea caves, the boulders on its far shelf, the fig's boughs and leaves, the cloud's puffs within its planned bodies, the birds' rounds, the
//     timber's place on its round and its arm, the phases of the ship's sway). The mountain, the shoal, the bluff, the fig's place, the gate, the line and the pace are plan.
//   The hour is fixed: ctx.vary.hour is not read.
//   NOTHING IS KEYED TO A STILL, A SEED OR THE LENS. What reads where the eye IS, named here: the shaders' view vector (the sea's mirror and sheen, with the rocks' doubles broken across by the waves along the look from the eye; the wet stone's sheen; the fog); the cards of the
//     spray and of the cloud turned to face the lens as they are drawn; the light that comes through thin vapour and the linings of the cloud's heads by the look's angle to the sun; the ship's own
//     eye law; the gate's haze and its lit edge against the sun (the view vector and the eye's distance: optics); the sun's blaze and rays seen through the cloud's vapour (the look's angle to the
//     sun, where the puff's own place is in the sun); the wet sheen on the shoal's ramps (the eye's angle to the stone); the gate's shaded stone darker where the eye looks toward the sun (the eye's
//     angle to the sun); AND THE FILTERS OF FINE DETAIL, each thing going to its own mean, said here exactly as they stand:
//       BY A PIXEL'S FOOTPRINT ALONE (the metres of the thing that one pixel covers at the thing's own place; no distance is typed): the pool's finest white water (the wall's streaks, the tongues, the
//         shaft's threads, the lip's strands, the rim's tear), the timber's grain, and THE SPRAY'S CARDS BY THEIR OWN DRAWN SIZE (a thrown drop or a puff of the low mist that would be drawn lower
//         than a pixel and a half is drawn that high and fainter by its area; from the card's size in the world, its depth before the lens, the lens's own scale and the picture's height in pixels).
//       BY A PIXEL'S FOOTPRINT AND, ON TOP OF IT, A TYPED SPAN OF THE EYE'S DISTANCE: the high wall's thin beds, and they alone (whole to 70 m, gone by 190 m).
//       BY A TYPED SPAN OF THE EYE'S DISTANCE ALONE, with no footprint term under it (a level of detail by where the eye is, against shimmer): the high wall's wet streaks (whole to 60 m, gone by
//         170 m); the sea's far slope (the swell's own slope in place of the mesh's, coming in from 120 to 260 m), its ripple (30 to 220 m), its five trains of wind waves (the longest whole to 70 m
//         and gone by 200 m, the shortest whole to 14 m and gone by 44 m) and its facets' three scales (150 to 600 m, 40 to 160 m, 10 to 45 m); the sand's ripples on the shelf under the pool (whole
//         to 60 m, gone by 140 m).
//     Of these readings none reads where the eye LOOKS and none hides a thing in the picture.
//     ONE NUMBER IS TYPED AGAINST THE PICTURE'S EDGE, named here in the open so that this rule claims no more than stands: GAZE_IN.clear, the pitch the turn onto the bow is carried at. It was typed
//     from where her foam's bands lie about the lens and where the picture's lower edge falls under the world's field (CAM_FOV): not from a still, a seed or a sheet, but from the lens's field all the
//     same (the first turn's GAZE_IN.over, riseFrom and riseTo, live's own, are of the same cause: see turnPitch). Her foam's mesh is no longer drawn at all (the site owner's cure of 11 Oct in the
//     ship's part), so the number no longer guards anything; it is kept as built by the site owner's word, because the round's deciding sheets were made with it.
//   WHAT IS TYPED so that it is seen well from the way, for the whole stretch and every seed, named here:
//     THE GATE'S ROW (the part's TAIL) IS LAID BY HAND: each of its thirteen slabs is a typed place, turn and height, laid so that it stands whole as seen from the way along the last straight, its broad face to the oncoming eye.
//     THE SHOAL'S LEDGES (the part's FANG_AT) ARE LAID BY HAND: each of its fourteen ledges is a typed place, height and breadth, laid so that they are seen well from the way in; one of them (FANG_AT[8], far to port) stood where, as the way turns onto the sun (about the metre 343, the look dead ahead), it bore 47 degrees to port of the bow, 80 m off, on the very rim of what the look ahead holds, a low bar half within it, and was moved 30 m north, where through that turn it bears 67 degrees and more to port: wholly aside of the look ahead.
//     And: the bluff's place and its shelf toward the line and the pool (the part's HORN), the fig's measures (the part's FIG_REACH, FIG_IN, FIG_LEAN, FIG_FLAT), the one dip of the shoal's beds and which
//     of its ledges are ramps (SHOAL_DIP, SHOAL_RAMPS: the ramps lie to starboard of the way in and their tops lean toward it), the shoal's gulls' ledges (SHOAL_GULLS), the cloud shelf's three rings
//     (SHELF), the gaze's named angles (GAZE_IN, GAZE_POOL, GAZE_GATE) and the ledges its first knots are named from (SHOAL_FIRST, SHOAL_NEAR, SHOAL_DOWN, SHOAL_BROAD, SHOAL_LAST), the gate's gulls'
//     slabs (GATE_TALL, GATE_GULLS), the ship's light (SHIP_LIGHT), the way out's radius (WAY_OUT_R), the three speeds and the named places they are eased between (PACE_IN, PACE_CROSS, PACE_SLOW,
//     makePace), the slabs between which the way out stands (GATE_LAST, GATE_BEYOND), the bird companies' places (birdPlan: each said there; the three crossing gulls' is found, midway between the two
//     rocks' built places), and the line itself (LINE: its first straight is laid down the lane of light through the gully G1-R1, found by plan from the sun's bearing; its last straight is laid at the
//     sun by the sun's own bearing, SUN_PORT).
//
// THE PLAN: the line's metre m is -z on its long straight (x -10, m 187.5 to 292); before it the straight in down the lane of light and the one bend to starboard, after it the bend to port and the
// last straight (see LINE and planAt in build). The mountain's nearest stone stands 29.5 m from her whole breadth (the metre 244), G1's foot centre at (-86, -196); the pool's centre at x 42, z -262;
// the fig's foot at (23.8, -316.9), 11.2 m up.
//
// THE WAY OUT (it stands for every seed). There are two rocks and no third. She leaves the strait by its southern mouth, through the gate. The way out is the kit's own, 7.5 m in radius, at the line's
// last place, square to its last heading, its foot in the sea: low broken water stands along its foot (AT EVERY METRE OF THE LINE AND EVERY SECOND: a standing thing of the place, which reads nothing of the way; no light of the opening lies in the water before it: the sea gives back the sky and the stone alone). THE RING ITSELF IS FIRST DRAWN, by the engine's own late drawing,
// WHERE THE SOUTHERN MOUTH OPENS: at the metre at which the last of the low rock's stone is abeam (M.shoulder, about the metre 320, some 101 m of the line before its end): up to there the low rock
// stands beside her; past its last stone nothing stands between her and the mouth, and the ring that stands in the mouth is drawn from then on. One rule of place; nothing of the picture is tested for it.
// It then stands with THE SUN OVER IT as the look comes onto the bow, between the gate's slabs until the last metres. The mountain's long shadow lies astern.
import * as THREE from 'three';
import { makePath, makeRng, hashSeed } from '../kit/core.js';
import {
  PC, SUN, ROCK_C, CRAG, MASSIF, BLOCKS, MASSIF_C, CAVE, SLAB, massifInside, massifShade, FUN, funnelAt, NAR0, NARL, narrows, TAIL, sst, GLSL_SEA, GLSL_SKY, GLSL_FOG, GLSL_NOISE, makeShared, makeSeaMaterial, makeStoneMaterial, seaGeometry, gridGeometry, bakeGrid, buildHero,
} from '../parts/strait-between-two-rocks.js';
import { tick, tock, first, FANGS, FANG_AT, fangsInside, fangsInsideAimed, massifInsideAimed, rockAim, stepNormals, prepareBodies, massifPast, sunEndOf, GLSL_SHADOW, flagsGeometry, SHOAL_DIP } from '../parts/strait-between-two-rocks.js';
import { buildShip } from '../parts/bronze-walled-island.js';

const TAU = Math.PI * 2, D2R = Math.PI / 180;
const clamp = (x, a, b) => Math.min(b, Math.max(a, x));
// THE LINE. Five pieces, the heading continuous: A a straight in, LINE.lead metres long, heading LINE.turn degrees to port of the long straight; B ONE BEND to starboard (LINE.R metres' radius) onto
// C, THE LONG STRAIGHT under the mountain's east flank (x = LINE.x; on it the line's metre m is -z, from m LINE.on to m LINE.out); D a bend TO PORT (LINE.side = -1; LINE.R2 metres' radius) of
// LINE.round degrees round the low rock's tree end; E a last straight of LINE.last metres to the way out.
// A IS LAID DOWN THE LANE OF LIGHT: by the plan's own march toward the sun from places on the sea, the sun is seen from the sea north-east of the mountain only down ONE LANE, through the gully
// between G1 and R1: its middle runs from (87.5, -40) through (71.0, -60) to (50.8, -84), 6 to 8 m broad to z -56, 1.9 m at z -72, shut by z -86. A passes through (71, -60) heading 40 degrees to
// port of the long straight (the lane's own run there), from its first metre 20.2 at (79.3, -50), so that for her first 44 m the sun's blaze stands in the gully ahead of her (the whole disc for
// 14 m, then a narrowing slit) and the mountain stands whole and backlit round it; LINE.turn = 40, LINE.R = 85, LINE.on = 187.5 and LINE.lead = 108 are typed from that (the bend begins at the
// metre 128.2 at (9.9, -132.9)). It holds on every seed (the mountain is plan).
// THE POEM'S SIDE: on C she keeps to the tall rock's side of the strait all the way down it, away from the pool (the mountain's east feet 33 m and less to port, the throat 52 m to starboard), and
// turns away round the low rock's tree end only when the pool is astern.
// D AND E ARE LAID AT THE SUN: D turns by THE SUN'S OWN BEARING to port of the long straight (LINE.round = SUN_PORT, 42.0 degrees; 85 m radius), from the metre 292 to 354.3; on E she heads at the
// sun: the hour's one sun stands over her bow, 13 degrees high, over the way out; its path on the sea runs up the straight to the way out's foot, and the gate's slabs stand to either hand of it
// (the part's TAIL). She leaves the strait by its southern mouth; the low rock's tree end and its fig pass to starboard, 31.6 m and more off, and are looked back at.
const SUN_PORT = Math.atan2(-SUN[0], -SUN[2]) / D2R;     // the sun's bearing to port of the long straight's bow (42.0 degrees; the part's SUN)
// THE LAST STRAIGHT'S LENGTH, BY PLACE: THE WAY OUT STANDS IN THE GATE'S LAST OPENING, midway (by the metres along the straight) between the last slab she passes (GATE_LAST: the part's TAIL[2], to
// port, 34.0 m along the straight) and the nearer of the pair that stands to either hand of the way beyond it (GATE_BEYOND: TAIL[4], 98.0 m along): the opening's middle, where one who has passed the
// last slab has as far still to go to the next. So the straight is 66.0 m long and the line's last metre is 420.3; the line's length and every place's share of the way are what this makes them.
const GATE_LAST = 2, GATE_BEYOND = 4;
const LINE_Y = 3.6, LINE = (() => { const L = { x: -10, turn: 40, R: 85, on: 187.5, lead: 108, out: 292, R2: 85, round: SUN_PORT, last: 0, side: -1 };
  const th = L.round * D2R, hx = L.side * Math.sin(th), hz = -Math.cos(th), x0 = L.x + L.side * L.R2 * (1 - Math.cos(th)), z0 = -L.out - L.R2 * Math.sin(th);     // the last straight's first place and its heading
  const along = (i) => (TAIL[i][0] - x0) * hx + (TAIL[i][1] - z0) * hz; L.last = 0.5 * (along(GATE_LAST) + along(GATE_BEYOND)); return L; })();
// (the lens rides 3.6 m over the standing sea and dips with the funnel's slope: over the HIGHEST standing water under her
// foredeck's plan (its outer edge, 1.5 m to port, from the lens to the stem head 5.74 m ahead): see lineY in build)

